# {{ names.sync_local }} (maintainer authoring helper)

Keep **{{ names.knowledge }}** in sync with the actual code under `{{ paths.repos_root }}`.
The writing conventions come from `{{ names.add_project }}` (per-project docs) and
`{{ names.add_domain }}` (domain flows); authority for all rules is `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md`, plus the rules specific to {{ identity.display_name }} in `../{{ names.local_rules }}.md`.

## Target paths

```
KNOWLEDGE={{ derived.knowledge_root }}   # adjust to your clone
$KNOWLEDGE/references/project/<name>.md  +  index.md
$KNOWLEDGE/references/domain/<topic>.md  +  index.md
REPOS={{ paths.repos_root }}/<repo>   # your local source repos
```

The full path set (the shared layer: `SKILL.md`{{#if has.observability_doc}} / `observability.md`{{/if}}{{#if has.shared_catalogs}} / `platform-basics.md`{{/if}}), the `$KNOWLEDGE` root, and all rules (§4 extract, §5 live-tool verify, §6 hard rules, §7 consistency, §10 audit/verify discipline) live in `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md` (source of truth). Edit the source repository, never an installed cache copy.

## Two modes

- **ADD** — the user names new project(s) not yet documented → research → write. Route: a single project → `{{ names.add_project }}`; a cross-project flow → `{{ names.add_domain }}`.
- **AUDIT / REFRESH** (the default when the user says "update / sync / is anything stale") → the loop below.

To review **someone else's changeset** to `references/` (a branch / MR / commit range) rather than sweep the whole kb, use `{{ names.verify }}` instead — the same verify-against-local-repos discipline, scoped to a diff, with explicit `(human:)`-fact handling.

## AUDIT loop — every doc, every point

**Every** `references/project/*.md` and `references/domain/*.md` is a manifest row. Nothing is sampled; a doc is skipped only by a rule named below (empty commit range, by-age fast-path, `Status: retired`), and each skip is recorded on the ledger's not-touched line. Spending extra tokens to verify each point is expected and wanted. **An empty base** (no project or domain doc yet) has no rows: skip Gate 0 and the manifest, report every in-scope repo as a coverage gap, and continue in ADD mode. **One exception, by rule and not by glob**: a project doc whose header carries `- Status: retired` is **not re-verified against code** — its repo may well still exist and still diff, but drift in a service nobody runs is not a finding. There, check only that the retirement record still holds (banner present, `## Retired` row in `project/index.md` intact) and move on.{{#if has.shared_catalogs}} The **shared-layer roll-up doc `references/platform-basics.md`** is reconciled at the end of the project sweep (see the shared-layer reconciliation step after the bidirectional-link audit) — it is not mapped to a single repo, so it is not read as a phase-1 per-doc target.{{/if}}

**Order matters at scale: project docs, then domain docs, then a reflow pass over whatever the first two disturbed** (see Fan-out → phased pipeline). Steps 1–7 below are the **project-doc** procedure (single repo + `Verified at` commit-range). **Domain docs run in phase 2** and follow a different procedure — no single repo / `Verified at`; they carry a date-only `Swept at` cursor (authoring-core §3/§7), consume the `downstreamChanges` digests emitted by their `Projects involved` (phase 1), and check their own cross-repo anchors. Step 1's repo-mapping still applies; steps 2–3/7's single-repo-cursor mechanics do not (the `Swept at` by-age fast-path replaces them — see "Fine-scoping").

### Gate 0 — anchor self-test BEFORE the manifest (mechanical, minutes, no judgment)

The manifest is built **from** the anchors block, and the whole pass uses anchors to locate code — so a broken anchor poisons everything downstream: a pattern that can never hit reports drift on every run forever, and a real rename hides behind that permanent noise. Rule + the MISS taxonomy: `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §9` (pilot mode). Run it first, fix what fails, **then** build the manifest. Gate 0 covers the `code claims` anchors only (the lines carrying a `grep:` pattern); the other classes carry no pattern and are scheduled by the manifest. Resolve each anchor's `<repo>` through step 1's repo map (below) before calling a repo uncloned.

**Scope it by what is already PROVEN, not by what is cheap — a full scan of every anchor line on every run re-proves what cannot have come undone.** Gate 0 exists to catch a **broken pattern**, not a genuine miss (a real rename is drift, and step 3b catches that per doc). So the property to cache is *non-vacuity*: an anchor that **hit** at its last Gate 0 and whose line **nobody has edited since** is proven legal and proven capable of matching — neither can lapse on its own, because both are properties of the pattern text plus one demonstrated hit.

Run the full check — both parts below, so (a) and the (b)/(c) pair — this pass only over:

- **anchor lines edited since the last Gate 0** — diff `references/` for edits on or after the header's `gate0-full` date (the cursor is date-only, so edits made later that same day count: a rename fix edits an anchor after Gate 0 ran); and
- **anchor lines that did not hit last time** — the header's `not-passing` list: still unproven, and the `unresolvable` pile too (a repo may have been cloned since).

Both cursors come from the header (first line) of the **most recent ledger in `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/`** — the newest file named exactly `<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`; a `-retro.md` note and the per-run `<date>/` working folder are not ledgers. The header's fields are defined in the ledger section below. **No ledger, or no header line in it → nothing is proven: run the complete Gate 0 and the run in full mode.** That is the first-ever run, a fresh checkout, and a ledger someone deleted — all three want the same conservative answer, and defaulting the other way would silently skip every anchor on the one run that has verified none of them.

Everything else carries its previous verdict. **Full mode re-runs the complete Gate 0**, which the per-run {{ audit.full_sweep_max_age_days }}d rule already forces, so every anchor is re-proven at least that often.

Report the counts as `checked (of <total>) / failed / unresolvable / carried` — the fourth number is what stops a scoped run from reading like a shrinking anchor base.

This does not weaken the "before the manifest" placement: the manifest is poisoned by a **newly broken** anchor, and one is broken by being written or edited — which is exactly the set above. (What neither the scoped nor the full gate catches: a pattern nobody edited that starts matching the **wrong** thing because the repo grew new code — §9's silent-mismatch shape. It is repo-dependent and only surfaces when the claim itself is verified, so it is not a cost of this scoping.)

Two parts, per anchor line, per `|`-alternative:

```bash
# (a) is the pattern a legal ERE at all?  rc=2 / stderr = broken, not a MISS
grep -qE "$pat" /dev/null

# (b) does it actually hit in its named repo?  (git grep = index-based, fast)
git -C {{ paths.repos_root }}/<repo> grep -lIE -e "$pat" | head -1

# (c) when (b) is 0 hits: is the literal text there and the regex ate it?
#     -E 0 + -F >0  = broken pattern (§9 ②/⑥/⑦).  both 0 = go read the code.
git -C {{ paths.repos_root }}/<repo> grep -lIF -e "$pat" | head -1
```

Splitting on `|` is **top-level only** — a `|` inside `(...)` belongs to the pattern (`/api(/|$)`), and cutting there invents a broken half that was never there.

**Three outcomes, not two** — collapsing them is how this gate would manufacture its own false work:
- **pass** — legal, and hits.
- **fail → fix the pattern** (never the claim, never the repo) — **but only the shapes §9 calls broken**. §9's other pile (a `Class.Method` composite that is two real symbols but no literal string; an inverted claim) is a *correct* MISS: it must not be counted here and must not be "fixed". Gate 0 does not re-decide that taxonomy, it applies it — when a non-hit does not obviously fall in one pile, read the code before touching the pattern.
- **unresolvable → skip, do not count as either**: (i) the named repo is **not cloned locally** (`git -C` fails — that is a partial-clone fact, not a bad pattern; family docs and any not-yet-cloned repo land here), or (ii) the anchor line **declares itself inverted** (absence IS the claim — §9 requires that label on the line, so a labelled one is correct; an **unlabelled** one is a fail of the LABEL, not of the pattern — §9 says to add the label, so it must not go into the fail bucket above, which would have you rewrite a pattern whose MISS is right). **"Declares" means §9's two machine-checkable forms only** (the uppercase token `INVERTED` on the line, or the line sitting below the `INVERTED anchors —` sub-block header), never a reading of the label's English. In one measured base, 15 labelled inverted anchors were counted as failures once per run until the rule was applied; counting them trains you to ignore the gate.

Parts (b) and (c) are a **pair, and neither may be dropped from the set being checked** — (c) only fires when (b) returns 0 hits, so removing (b) silently disables (c) as well: an unescaped `(` / `{` makes a pattern **parse fine and still never match** code that is plainly there, so checking legality alone misses it, and (c) is what tells that apart from a genuine absence *without reading anything* (measured numbers: `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §9` pilot mode). Report the counts (above: `checked (of <total>) / failed / unresolvable / carried`) so a silent 0-failure run is distinguishable from a scan that broke, and so a growing `unresolvable` count surfaces as a clone gap rather than hiding as a pass. **Count them per anchor LINE, not per (alternative, repo) pair** — the unit is what decides whether this gate hands back 2 items or 138 of which 136 are phantom; the rule and the two shapes a genuine all-miss can take are in `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §9`.

### Manifest first — triage cheaply, then verify (the lever on BOTH round-trips and tokens)

**Before reading any doc in full, build the audit manifest** — one cheap pass that greps every doc's `<!-- anchors -->` block, header dates (`Verified at` / `Swept at` / `Last updated`), and inline markers. No full-doc reads yet. Classify every claim into the work-list:

- **code anchor whose commit-range was touched** (project: pickaxe `git log -G'<pat>' Verified-at..origin/DEF`) → verify this pass (cheap).
- **runtime anchor past its by-age threshold** (`noncode` {{ audit.by_age_days.noncode_refs }}d{{#if has.global_settings}} / `global-setting values` {{ audit.by_age_days.global_setting_values }}d{{/if}}{{#if has.logs}} / `liveness probes` {{ audit.by_age_days.liveness_probes }}d{{/if}} — authoring-core §9) → probe yourself (orchestrator).{{#if has.global_settings}} A `global-setting values` line asserting a mechanism also gets its key's code reader grepped in the same probe (§9 — the value holding does not mean anything reads it).{{/if}}{{#if has.observability_doc}} Read `references/observability.md`'s query recipes before the first live query of the run.{{/if}}
- **`(human:)` / `(external:)` marker** → NEVER verify — this is the **irreducible-open** set (structurally unverifiable here), and it is the ONLY thing that legitimately remains open at the end.
- **fresh + untouched + within by-age** → skip; record it on the wave's not-touched ledger line.

**Full re-sweep override.** The by-age skips (domain `Swept at` fast-path, runtime-anchor `verified:`-age) are *heuristics* — the user can turn them OFF for one run by **just saying so in the invocation** (`full sync` / `trust nothing` / `ignore fast-path` / `re-verify everything` — or `/{{ names.sync_local }} full`). It's a prompt-level mode, no flag/code needed. In full mode: re-verify **every** doc's content and re-run **every** runtime anchor probe regardless of `Swept at` / `Verified at` / `verified:` freshness. The one skip still honored is the project-doc **empty-commit-range** skip (git proving 0 commits since `Verified at` is a *fact*, not a guess — re-reading those is pure waste; to override even that, the user says "re-read every doc" explicitly). Reach for full mode after a big cross-repo refactor or when trust matters more than tokens — and **enter it without being asked when the last full sweep was more than {{ audit.full_sweep_max_age_days }}d ago** (authoring-core §9): that is the per-run half of the backstop, not a preference. Say so when you do. The date is the `full-sweep` field in the header of the most recent ledger in `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/` (a scoped run's own file date is not a full sweep); **no ledger, or no header = no known full sweep = full mode**.

The manifest **is this audit's completeness contract** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10`): DONE = every row verified-this-pass or in the irreducible-open set; front-load full scope + finish line to the user in one shot. Why it matters most at sync scale: the round-trip cost is the single biggest lever on wall-clock and token — in a long session each extra round-trip re-processes the entire context, which dwarfs the verification work itself (so resolve-don't-defer, §10, is not just correctness but throughput here).

Per doc (project):

1. **Map to repo(s)** — from the `repo:` header (project docs) or the linked `../project/*.md` (domain docs). **The doc filename ≠ the local dir name** — resolve the local `{{ paths.repos_root }}/<dir>` from the `repo:` URL's last path segment{{#if has.repo_rules}}, applying the configured transforms ({{ lists.repo_rules }}){{/if}}{{#if has.repo_aliases}} and the explicit aliases where the doc name and repo diverge ({{ lists.repo_aliases }}){{/if}}. The map is rendered from `paths.repo_dir_map` in `knowledge.config.json` (in `{{ paths.repo }}`); when you meet a new mismatch, apply it by hand for this run and record it there so the next render carries it. Family docs map to no single dir — that is not a gap. If the repo genuinely no longer exists at the expected path → flag as **possibly renamed/moved/removed** (don't delete the doc; surface it). **Non-git docs** (`repo:` says the code is mirrored from another source-control system) → audit by reading code, **skip the git-diff steps, do not flag the missing remote**.
2. **Refresh local view** — `git -C $REPO fetch --quiet` (do NOT pull/merge — leaves the user's working tree alone; compare against `origin/<default>`).
3. **Find what changed since the doc was verified** (the `Verified at` sha IS the audit cursor — exact commit diff, no date fuzziness):
   ```bash
   DEF=$(git -C "$REPO" symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD 2>/dev/null | sed 's#origin/##')   # the default branch
   [ -z "$DEF" ] && DEF=$(git -C "$REPO" branch -r | grep -Eo 'origin/(main|master)' | head -1 | sed 's#origin/##')   # fallback if origin/HEAD unset
   TRK=$(sed -nE 's/^- Tracks branch: \**`?([^`* ]+).*/\1/p' "$DOC"); [ -n "$TRK" ] && DEF=$TRK   # doc describes a non-default branch (authoring-core §3) — diff that branch, not the default
   BASE=$(sed -nE 's/^- Verified at: ([0-9a-f]+).*/\1/p' "$DOC")              # the stored commit anchor
   [ -z "$BASE" ] && BASE=$(git -C "$REPO" rev-list -1 --first-parent --before="<Last updated> 23:59" "origin/$DEF")   # fallback: old doc with no/`n/a` Verified at → derive from Last updated
   git -C "$REPO" log   --oneline "$BASE..origin/$DEF"
   git -C "$REPO" diff  --stat    "$BASE..origin/$DEF"
   ```
   **Empty `log` with exit 0 = doc still current against latest** → no content review needed; just refresh `Verified at` (step 7). Non-empty → those are the only commits to assess. A non-zero exit (a `$BASE` that no longer exists after rewritten history, a shallow clone) is not an empty range: fall back to the `Last updated` derivation above and say so in the ledger.
   **3b. Anchors fast-path** (if the doc has an `<!-- anchors -->` block — see `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §9`): for each `code` anchor, `grep -rE "<pattern>" <repo>` — a hit **locates** the current code to read; a **MISS (symbol renamed/removed) = drift flag**. This only locates — it never replaces step 3. The authoritative signal stays the **commit-range diff** (step 3), and **symbol-present ≠ behavior-still-correct**: when the diff shows the anchored area changed, read it. For `noncode` anchors, sort by `verified:` date and surface the oldest for manual re-check (workflow tools / external pages / DB DDL never appear in a code diff). For `snapshot tables` anchors (an inlined value table — the enum/value snapshot carve-out in `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §0` / §9), **re-read the `source:` file and regenerate the table rows per `derive:`, then diff against the inlined table** — any missing/changed row (append-only enums mainly *gain* rows) is drift → update the table + its `(snapshot <date>, source …)` tag. This re-derivation **is sync, not a format pass** — a plain symbol-grep can't catch a table that went stale by omission, so do it whenever the doc is audited. For a **structural skeleton** (a flow/pipeline/state-machine mermaid — `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §0`), **diff the skeleton's nodes against the prose stage sequence / branches**: a node with no matching prose stage (or a prose stage the diagram lost) is **structural drift** → fix the skeleton to match prose (prose is authority); an identifier/value that crept into a node → strip it back to structure. Within-doc consistency (no code source to re-derive from), cheap — the skeleton is coarse; do it whenever such a doc is audited.{{#if has.logs}} For `liveness probes` anchors, **re-run the log/metric query yourself** (orchestrator-only) — sustained 0 over the window is a **retirement-review flag, not drift and not a deletion** (see step 5 retirement; zero in a log system ≠ dead).{{/if}}
4. **Decide depth from the changed files**:
   - Touched **doc-relevant** files (runtime config, deploy manifests, dependency manifests, CI config, the container build file, startup/registration code, RPC definitions, route definitions) → **re-verify the affected sections** (the sweep prompt in `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §4`, run by a fan-out agent){{#if has.live_tools}} + live tools (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §5`){{/if}}.
   - Only unrelated source changed → still read the whole doc and sanity-check each claim against current code; correct anything that no longer holds.
5. **Reconcile** — update stale facts, add genuinely new ones, drop things that are gone. Keep ownership as recorded unless the **user** changes it (never re-derive from a catalog file or a deployment annotation — see hard rules).
   - **Additive-candidate scan (read the range for what to ADD, not only what drifted).** The `Verified at..origin/DEF` diff is already in hand for step 3 — scan the SAME diff for **doc-worthy facts the range introduced that the doc doesn't yet cover**, don't only check whether existing claims still hold. This turns "additions" from a tiring push (the maintainer brainstorming what might be missing) into a pull (the delta surfaces candidates), at near-zero extra cost (no extra fetch, scope bounded to the delta — far cheaper than a whole-repo re-scan). Candidate bar = **authoring-core §0** ("only what code can't tell you in 10 min"): a new upstream dependency, a new message topic / RPC definition, a new endpoint group or capability, a new background job, a new env / deployment / platform, a new cross-project seam, or a reference to a **project that has no doc yet** (→ ADD-mode candidate). NOT every new file — filter noise by the §0 bar. **Cap to the few highest-value candidates per doc** (same discipline as the per-agent proposal cap, Scale control item 3) — a per-doc flood of low-value additions is the blow-up failure again, just from the additive side. Output candidates as **leads, not final text** (same "contribution is a lead" discipline, `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`): surface them, the user decides what to write. **Bounds:** the delta of an *existing* repo won't reveal a brand-new **repo** (that's the whole-inventory coverage-gaps check below) and won't catch **cross-repo omission** (A gains a role in a flow while you audit C's doc — that needs the identifier sweep + `downstreamChanges`, §10 omission / contract item 9). It DOES catch new modules/services inside a monorepo and a dependency pointing at an undocumented project.
   - **Doc-level retirement (bounds the growth of what must be verified).** Dropping a gone *fact* is routine; when a whole **flow / subject** is dead{{#if has.logs}} — its `liveness probes` anchor is dry over the window **and** no live code path backs it{{else}} — no live code path backs it{{/if}} — escalate from fact-editing to proposing the doc be **archived or merged** (same spirit as the retired-standard hard rule for dead projects). Never auto-delete: a dry probe is a review flag (zero in a log system ≠ dead — environment-tag quirks, low volume, prod logging to a non-prod stream). Surface it; the user decides retire vs keep.
6. **Conform format** to the current `{{ names.add_project }}` / `{{ names.add_domain }}` template (section names, env table rules, markers) — fix drift while here. A **domain doc** in an old format (a blockquote header, top-level `## Flow A` not under a `## Flows` mother section, bare-path links) → migrate to the current schema (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §7`): meta-list header, `## Flows` mother section, markdown links. While migrating, apply **verify-or-drop** to any `## TODO` (§6) — verify against the local repo, or drop if minor.
7. **Bump the header**:
   - **`Verified at`** — **bumped last, and only once every finding on the doc is applied, refuted, or listed as flagged for the user**: a doc whose verifier crashed or whose finding is still pending keeps its old cursor, so the next run's range still contains the drift (a cursor moved past unverified work buries it permanently). Then refresh to the commit just diffed against (`git -C "$REPO" rev-parse --short "origin/$DEF"` + its `%cs` date), **even when content was unchanged** — the doc is now confirmed good against that newer commit, so the next audit's window starts there (this is what keeps re-audits cheap). For non-git docs keep `n/a (<reason>)`.
   - **`Last updated`** — bump to today **only if the doc content actually changed**. If verified clean and unchanged, leave the date as-is.
   - **`Swept at`** (domain docs only — the `Verified at` analogue) — refresh to today whenever the domain doc was re-verified this pass, whether by a full re-read OR a qualified by-age fast-path bump (see Fine-scoping), under the same bump-last rule as `Verified at`. Date-only (no sha). A domain doc with no `Swept at` yet → full re-read, then add it — same organic backfill as project docs that lacked `Verified at`.

Then: **coverage gaps** — list repos under `{{ paths.repos_root }}` that are in scope under the rule "{{ scope.rule }}" but have **no doc** → propose adding via ADD mode. The symmetric direction: **retirement candidates** — docs whose subject is dead (a dry liveness probe + no live code path, or a `possibly renamed/moved/removed` repo) → propose archive/merge (step 5 retirement). ADD grows coverage, retirement bounds it — report both.

Also: **bidirectional-link audit** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`) — for every `domain/<slug>.md`, each project under `Projects involved` must have a back-link bullet in its project doc's **`## Flows` section** (`- **<Flow>** — [\`slug\`](../domain/slug.md)`), and vice versa. Any one-directional link = drift → fix it. Old docs may still carry a **deprecated header blockquote** (`> Part of the … flow`) — migrate those to a `## Flows` section during audit. **Do this orchestrator-side, not inside a per-doc agent** — a single-doc agent only holds one end and will mis-call link state (real cases: an agent claimed a `## Flows` section was missing when it wasn't, and claimed a back-link existed when it didn't). Grep both sides yourself across all domain + project docs. Match the section headings by **prefix** (`^## Projects involved`, `^## Flows`) — some carry a suffix (`## Projects involved (each fetches from …)`), and an exact `$`-anchored match once reported 9 false gaps.

Also: **omission sweep for domain docs** — the identifier sweep defined in `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10` ("Omission sweep", including the live-step bar), orchestrator-side because only this full clone can run it. For each domain doc read in full this pass (not fast-pathed), grep its message topics / client names / RPC services / endpoints / procedure names across every clone under `{{ paths.repos_root }}`. A repo that handles one and is absent from `Projects involved` is a **MISSING-PROJECT lead** in the report; the user decides whether to add it (ADD mode, like a coverage gap). This is the "`Projects involved` completeness" gate that the Barrier note below names.
{{#if has.shared_catalogs}}

Also: **shared-layer roll-up reconciliation** (`references/platform-basics.md`, authoring-core §9 `snapshot tables`) — this doc is a denormalised roll-up of facts that live (and are audited) in the individual project docs ({{ derived.catalog_headings }}: each is the union of every project's matching field line, grouped), so it is kept fresh by reconciling, not by hand. **Orchestrator-side, at the end of the project sweep** (the per-project facts are already verified this pass): re-derive each catalog from the audited project docs' field lines and diff against `platform-basics.md` — any project that moved, newly uses or dropped something this sweep but is not reflected = catalog drift → fix the table. **Diff-and-reconcile, not a destructive full regenerate**: only act on the projects that changed this sweep; leave catalog notes that did not change intact. Bump its `Swept at` when reconciled. This is near-zero extra cost (the source facts are already in hand from phase 1) and is what makes the catalog auto-fresh instead of a hand-maintained island. Keep the reader `SKILL.md`{{#if has.observability_doc}} and `observability.md`{{/if}} out of the per-claim loop (hand-curated shared layer); only `platform-basics.md` carries a re-derivable roll-up.
{{/if}}

Last: **self-consistency pass over this run's own edits** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10`, the second axis) — the `Verified at` / `Swept at` cursors track whether *upstream* moved, so they are blind to a doc contradicting itself; that arrives from a **doc edit**. Scope is therefore this run's own touched set, which is a closed list you already have. Take it in a way that includes **uncommitted** work — mid-run is the normal state, and `<base>..HEAD` alone silently drops everything not yet committed: `git diff --name-only <base> -- '*references*'` (working tree vs the run's start point) plus `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard -- '*references*'` for docs added this run, or `git status --short` if you have not committed at all this run. For each: re-read the **whole file**, not the diff — the other half of a contradiction is almost always in the part you did *not* touch (a summary-table row, a `Projects involved` bullet, `Key rules`, the glossary, an anchor name). It has to be a separate closing pass, not something done while writing: catching a contradiction in a paragraph you wrote ten minutes ago is genuinely harder than with a run's distance. A doc this pass actually corrects has **changed content**, so step 7's header rule applies again — bump its `Last updated` (and `Swept at` for a domain doc) rather than leaving the date the earlier step wrote. Docs edited by *earlier* runs that never got this pass are a backlog — work it oldest-`Last updated` first, and report it separately rather than folding it into this run's set.

## Fan-out (thoroughness at scale)

A full knowledge base is large. Audit it by fanning out **one agent per doc/repo** — a write-capable type, not Explore: Explore has no Write tool, and the finder and verifier below both write files (group docs that share a repo into one agent so the diff is read once — a monorepo backing several docs, a repo family). Each agent's conclusion (stale points + proposed edits) lands in its report file and only a pointer comes back (the wave gate below), never file dumps. For a complete sweep this is large; if the user has opted into multi-agent orchestration, a workflow is the right vehicle.

### Scale control — never fan out the whole kb blind

A naive "one agent per doc over every doc" is the **blow-up** failure: huge token cost, dozens of agents, and an output too big to review. Bound it:

1. **Cheap triage BEFORE any agent.** Pure-bash filters cut the fan-out set first: the empty-commit-range skip (step 3 — in one measured sweep it dropped 78 of 132 docs), and for a coverage/backfill pass a `grep` for which docs even lack an anchor block. Spawn agents only for what survives triage. Fanning out before triaging is the most common cause of blow-up.
2. **Run in capped WAVES, and a wave boundary is a checkpoint — NOT a question.** {{ lists.wave_size }} docs per wave; let the wave's verifiers apply, **close the wave gate below (spot-check, merge the ledger rows)**, then **start the next wave in the same turn**. Do NOT launch all surviving docs at once: a bad prompt or a wrong systematic assumption is caught after one wave, not after the whole set. Report progress per wave so the user *can* stop you — but **never ask permission to continue**. "Shall I continue with the next batch?" is the failure this rule exists to kill: batching is how you bound blast radius, not a reason to hand the run back. The run ends when the manifest has no rows left, not when the user answers. **"In the same turn" is literal, and a silent stop breaks it as surely as a question does**: the agents run in the background, so a wave's stragglers still reporting is not a reason to hand the turn back — apply what has landed, dispatch the next wave, then report. What batching buys is a gate on the **prompt shape**, and two or three usable waves prove that shape; from there, draining the current wave before dispatching the next one's finders costs wall-clock and buys nothing. Finders edit nothing, so overlapping them is safe; a wave's verifiers apply edits, and no later wave's verifier starts before that wave's gate has closed. Only a genuine fork you cannot resolve (§10 resolve-don't-defer: an `(external:)` owner call, a real scope ambiguity) stops the run — and that is put as a question, not as a menu.
3. **Cap proposals per agent.** Tell each agent to propose only the **highest-value / seam** items, not to anchor or flag every sentence (a backfill sweep once produced 31–35 proposals on single docs — mostly noise). A per-doc cap keeps review tractable and the doc un-bloated.
4. **Match effort to the task — but not below the floor the task needs.** High effort is for judgment (drift verify, the adversarial gate). Pure extraction (`snapshot tables` re-derive) can run cheap. **Anchor backfill is the trap**: at low effort agents emit unusable grep patterns — file paths, repo names, generic words (`Controllers`, `Services`), or whole code fragments with spaces/parens — which "match" but anchor nothing (a grep that can never miss never flags a rename). If you run anchor backfill at low effort you MUST (a) constrain the output format in the prompt — *"grep = pipe-separated clean grep symbols: CamelCase type/method names, dotted namespaces, routes, message topics — NEVER file paths, repo names, or generic dir words"* — AND (b) post-filter every proposed pattern: strip non-symbol tokens, then `grep`-verify what remains actually matches; drop anchors left with no real symbol. Without both, a low-effort backfill produces a doc full of anchors that look like coverage and detect nothing — worse than none. When in doubt, run backfill at normal effort.
5. **Scope to leverage, defer the tail.** Prioritise high-commit/active repos and substantial docs; a 13-line stub or a quiet repo can wait. Say what you deferred — don't silently treat a partial sweep as complete: a deferred doc is a manifest row left open, so the closing report names it and its one next action is the run that takes it.
6. **Cap concurrent agents at {{ audit.fan_out.max_concurrent_agents }}, and make a crashed agent's edits detectable.** In one past run, 8 agents launched together all died at the session rate limit (429), leaving 4 docs with partial edits and no ledger row — found only by luck. So: never more than {{ audit.fan_out.max_concurrent_agents }} in flight, finders and verifiers of every open wave counted together (a wave is a batch of docs, not a concurrency level: its groups queue behind the cap); record `git diff --stat -- <the docs it may touch>` before each dispatch, so a dead agent's partial edits show as a diff against that snapshot; and an editing agent appends its ledger row in the same step as each edit, not at the end of its run. A dead agent's partial edits are handed to a verifier as unverified findings (refute, keep what holds), never accepted as they stand.

### The wave gate — a wave is not closed until its own edits are verified

**An applied finding is still a finding** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10`: roughly a third of "simple" agent findings are wrong, and the second pass runs before the unit that produced them closes). Here the unit is the wave: verify **each wave's own diff, inside that wave**, not the accumulated diff of every wave at the end. A wave that applies its agents' output and rolls on writes wrong facts at a third the rate it writes right ones, which is what turns a next-session "is everything fine?" into a pile instead of one or two; spread across the run, the same total work is done where the context still holds the evidence.

**The agent that found it never verifies it** (§10, who runs the second pass). The orchestrator re-verifying every finding itself does not scale: in one past run, reports piled up faster than one context could gate them. So the default pipeline per doc group is three roles, all handing off through files:

1. **Finder** — audits, edits nothing, writes its report to `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<date>/reports/<group>.md` and returns only a one-line pointer. Reports kept in the orchestrator's context are what bloated that past run.
2. **Verifier** — a separate agent given the report path, briefed to **refute** each finding. It applies only what survives, runs the per-edit checks below on every edit it applies, and appends ledger rows to its own `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<date>/ledger-<group>.md`.
3. **Orchestrator** — spot-checks 2–3 claims per verifier against the cited `file:line`, weighted to negative claims and counts, then merges that verifier's rows into the run ledger. A failed spot-check re-opens that verifier's whole batch.

In that past run verifiers refuted several finder claims and several orchestrator inputs, which is the gate working.

Per wave, before it closes — every applied edit, no sampling (the verifier's checklist; the orchestrator runs it on whatever it applies itself):

- **Re-read the evidence, don't trust the quote.** Open the `file:line` the agent gave and confirm it says what the edit now asserts. A verbatim quote inside an agent report is not evidence that the line exists.
- **Run the trap checks the fan-out misses already name**, because they recur: every set-site / decorator / override / fallback traced (never the first grep hit); every count re-counted (never "≈"); every **negative** claim ("no autoscaling", "no Flows section") re-grepped independently; the repo actually searched is the repo the claim is about.
- **Filter the convention false positives before they become edits**: `Last updated` earlier than `Verified at` is correct, not drift; owner is never edited from a catalog file or deployment annotation; unmarked prose means code-verified, not unverified.
- **A claim that survives refutation is applied and logged; one that does not is reverted in the same wave**, while the context that produced it is still here. Reverting three waves later costs re-reading all of it again. Undo the edit itself; never `git checkout` / `git restore` a whole doc, which would also discard uncommitted work the file carried before the run.

A wave that cannot close its gate does not roll into the next one — fix or revert first. This gate is what lets the closing pass stay thin, so do **not** also defer these checks to the end.

### The ledger — write the evidence to disk, per wave

A long sweep gets compacted. Anything the run knows only in context is gone by the time anyone asks about it, which is why a next-session check would otherwise **re-derive the whole run's work** instead of checking it — slow, batched, and finding "new" things that were simply never recorded.

So each wave, as it closes its gate, **appends** to `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md` (relative to `{{ paths.repo }}`; create the directory on the first run). Run `git check-ignore -q {{ derived.ledger_dir }}/probe` once: when it reports not ignored, append `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/` to `.gitignore` and say so — the ledger survives the session and is never committed. One row per applied edit:

| doc | claim (old → new) | verified against | how | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `example-service.md` | retry window 7d → 14d | `{{ paths.repos_root }}/example-service` `Services/RetryService.cs:88` | `traced` (2 set-sites) | `held` |

`how` and `verdict` are **fixed vocabularies**, because the next-session check sorts on them — free text there and no two runs are comparable. `how` ∈ `traced` (every set-site / decorator / override / fallback followed) · `read` (one site opened, nothing else could set it) · `counted` · `grepped-absent` (a negative claim) · `probed` (a live-tool query). `verdict` ∈ `held` · `refuted` (then the edit is reverted in the same wave, and the row stays — a refuted row is the record that the gate worked). A bare `grepped` is not a value: a positive hit that was opened is `read` or `traced`, an absence is `grepped-absent`.

Plus a line per wave for what was **not** touched and why (empty range / pickaxe quiet + good coverage / `Status: retired` / `(human:)` / `(external:)`), because "we deliberately skipped it" and "we forgot it" are indistinguishable afterwards, and that ambiguity is what makes someone ask for the whole thing to be re-verified.

The ledger's **first line is its header**, a fixed line the next run parses: `gate0-full: <YYYY-MM-DD> | full-sweep: <YYYY-MM-DD> | mode: full|scoped | not-passing: <doc>#<claim label>; … (or none)`. `gate0-full` is the date of the last **complete** Gate 0: today's date when this run ran the complete one, otherwise copied from the previous ledger. `full-sweep` is the date of the last full-mode run that reached its closing report: set at close, otherwise copied. `not-passing` is the anchor lines that did not pass (`failed` + `unresolvable`), identified by doc and claim label because line numbers move; a scoped run carries the previous list, minus what now passes, plus what now fails. Record the failures, not the passes — the passing set is the large one and is recoverable by omission, while "did not hit last time" is precisely what Gate 0's scoping reads to decide what to re-prove. Without this the next run cannot tell a proven anchor from an unproven one and falls back to scanning all of them. The header is written when Gate 0 closes (so a crash after it still leaves the cursors) and replaced in place by a second run on the same date.

Appended per wave, never buffered to the end: the point is that it survives a compact, and a ledger written at the end is written from the same context a compact may already have dropped.

### Resuming — after a crash, a compact, or a second run on the same date

A re-run rebuilds the manifest from the docs, and a doc a wave already finished drops out on its own: its `Verified at` / `Swept at` moved, so its range now reads empty. What does not survive that move is everything derived from the old range, so read `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<date>/` before triage:

- `reports/*.md` and `additive-*.md` — a `downstreamChanges` digest found there counts as emitted this pass: feed Phase 2 and Phase 3 from it. The project doc behind it now looks quiet, and without the digest its domain docs would take the fast-path on a pass that saw their upstream change.
- `ledger-*.md` not yet merged into `<date>.md` — merge them; a doc with rows there is finished and is not re-audited.
- A report with no verifier rows — its verifier never ran: dispatch it. Nothing from that report is applied unrefuted.
- A doc with a working-tree diff and no ledger row — a dead agent's partial edits: a verifier refutes them like any other finding.

### The next-session check — spot-check the ledger, do not re-run the sweep

When someone asks "did yesterday's run check everything?" in a later session, **that is a check of the ledger, not a second audit.** Read `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<date>.md` plus any `<date>/ledger-*.md` the run never merged — a run that crashed or compacted between a verifier's write and the merge leaves its rows only there, and reading the run ledger alone turns each of them into a false "changed claim with no row". Then:

- **Reconcile the ledger against the diff** — every **changed claim** in the run's diff traces to a row, and every row landed in the diff. Reconcile on claims rather than lines: one edit routinely rewrites several lines, so a line-count comparison manufactures findings in exactly the check that is supposed to come back quiet. A changed claim with no row is the finding — it is the shape a claim takes when it was written without being verified. Header-only edits (`Verified at` / `Swept at` / `Last updated`) assert nothing and need no row.
- **Re-verify a sample of rows** (say 10, weighted to counts, negative claims, and anything whose `how` says "read" rather than "traced") by opening the cited `file:line`. A row that does not hold means the gate leaked — then, and only then, widen.
- **Confirm the skip lines are still true** — a doc skipped for an empty range should still show an empty range.

**Re-deriving the sweep from scratch is the wrong shape and costs the run again**; if the ledger is missing or too thin to reconcile, say that plainly — the fix is the next run keeping it, not a full re-audit now.

**Verification is an integral stage, never an afterthought** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10` — find → adversarially re-verify → propose, never find → apply; a finding is a proposal until a second pass confirms it). Audit in named batches and report progress — but the find/verify split still holds.

**Write the convention brief once, as a file, and hand its path to every agent** — finder, verifier, and consistency agents alike: `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<date>/brief.md`, written at run start (a `{{ names.verify }}` run building prompts from this contract writes the same file there for its own date). It carries: what `Last updated` (doc-edit date) vs `Verified at` (audit-cursor sha) mean (step 7; `Last updated` legitimately precedes `Verified at`, that is NOT drift); owner is flag-only (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`); the allowed markers incl. `(human:)` / `(external:)` (§0); the environments to list ({{ lists.env_names }}{{#if has.excluded_envs}}, never {{ lists.env_excluded }}{{/if}}); the §9 inverted-anchor marker; the ledger's fixed `how` / `verdict` vocabularies. A generic Explore agent without this manufactures convention-class drift: real sweeps produced "`Last updated` precedes `Verified at` = drift", owner edits taken from a deployment annotation, an excluded environment row, and `how` = `grepped` — each prompt restating its own subset is how the subset each agent got drifted.

**Agent output is a lead, not a verdict — the fan-out corollary of `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10`'s adversarial re-verify** (agents unreliable on cross-file / cross-doc / negative claims / first-grep-hit; ~30% of "simple" findings wrong; risk-tiering which to re-verify is itself unreliable). Concrete fan-out misses to expect — all fixed the same way: **trace the full call-graph (every set-site / decorator / override / fallback), match the doc text verbatim before editing**:
- a TTL a decorator overrides (the agent saw the startup default of 10m and missed the decorator's 1h);
- a value also set elsewhere (saw `CacheTime=5min`, missed `FromHours(1)` in two other files);
- an exact count (said 54, real 55; "≈20 areas" was 21);
- a wrong-repo search (searched the frontend repo for a backend claim);
- a **negative** claim ("no autoscaling", "no Flows section" — usually under-grepped).

Tell agents to **separate format/anchors gaps from factual drift** in their report, **give exact counts (never "≈20")**, **not stop at the first grep hit (trace every set-site / decorator / override / fallback)**, and **not propose adding new `<!-- anchors -->` blocks or reordering sections** — that is enhancement, not sync; only fix drift unless the user asks for a format pass. (This bans *format* enhancement — it does NOT ban surfacing new *facts*: reporting `additiveCandidates` as leads per contract item 10 is in scope, since the agent reports them, it does not edit them in.) **Exception — `snapshot tables` anchors are agent-doable**: re-deriving an inlined table from its `source:` code file is a concrete positive extraction (read file → list rows), reliable for agents — unlike live-tool runtime values (orchestrator-only, agents can't reach the tools). Have each agent re-derive any `snapshot tables` anchor in its doc and report row drift as factual drift, not a format gap.
{{#if has.logs}}

**Log-identity re-verification is the orchestrator's job** — agents can't reach {{ derived.labels.logs }}, and runtime values never appear in a code diff (that's what §9 `noncode refs` by-age is for). Don't let an agent block on it; re-check a log identity yourself only when the diff actually touched the logging configuration **or a deployment was added** (a new deployment can bring its own identity). It is also agent-doable to *pre-screen* from code: a local grep of the logging configuration tells you whether the repo ships logs to a sink, so an agent can flag a missing log bullet for you to probe once.
{{/if}}

**Verify to the depth the claim asserts; never hand a verifiable claim back to the user** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10` — the three-layer ladder code-present < behavior-traced < runtime-live, resolve-don't-defer, "a wording edit that asserts behavior is itself a claim", and "another kb doc is not a verification source"). At sync scale the payoff is concrete: punting a verifiable claim is the top cause of an audit that "keeps surfacing new unverified things" — each round just re-discovers what you deferred.
{{#if has.logs}}

**Batch the liveness probes into the audit; don't defer to a later prompt.** For every domain/flow doc carrying a `liveness probes` anchor, re-run its query in the same pass. This is how the verify surface stays bounded rather than growing every audit: a dry probe (+ no live code path) feeds **doc-level retirement** (step 5), so dead docs leave instead of accumulating.
{{/if}}

### Fan-out agent prompt contract (the skill is yours, not the subagent's)

A fanned-out agent has **none of this skill loaded** — it is a blank agent. Everything it needs you must put in its prompt. Resolve facts orchestrator-side; never make the agent guess. Every fan-out prompt MUST carry:

1. **Resolved repo path(s)** — the absolute `{{ paths.repos_root }}/<dir>`, already transformed per step 1 (a monorepo backing several docs → that one directory). Never pass the doc filename and let the agent infer the dir.
2. **The explicit commit range** `"<anchor>..origin/<DEF>"` — not "find the anchor yourself". (Project docs only: a domain doc has no single repo or range, its inputs are the `downstreamChanges` digests of Phase 2.)
3. **The doc path(s)** to audit, and that **its final text is the source of truth to diff against** (match verbatim before proposing an edit).
4. **Doc-relevant file list** to focus the diff (runtime config, deploy manifests, dependency manifests, CI config, the container build file, startup/registration code, RPC definitions, route definitions) — plus "but a claim can live in ordinary source; check the actual code, not just these".
5. **Convention brief** — the path to the run's `brief.md` (above), with the instruction to read it first; unmarked = code-verified.
6. **Tool boundary** — "you cannot reach the live tools; list any live-value claim (a log identity{{#if has.global_settings}}, a setting value{{/if}}) for the orchestrator, do not block on it. Also flag any **usage/liveness** assertion in the doc ('legacy', 'primary path', 'unused', 'still active') — you can confirm the code exists but NOT that prod exercises it; hand those to the orchestrator to probe, don't rubber-stamp them from code."
7. **Output contract** — under the wave gate's file handoff the report goes to its file and only a pointer returns, so the transit cap below binds whatever an agent still returns inline. **State a hard character cap in the prompt** and say the report is truncated in transit past it. Start from {{ audit.fan_out.agent_report_char_cap }} characters — that is an **observed harness limit, not a documented one**, so treat a fresh truncation as the limit having moved and re-derive it rather than trusting the number. Without a cap, a thorough agent writes a report you never receive: a real sweep lost 4 of 13 first-wave reports this way, and one agent had to be re-asked four times, each round-trip costing a turn. When a report does come back truncated, do not re-ask for the whole thing — re-ask for the missing sections only, in a fixed one-line-per-finding shape, with a smaller cap. Then: separate factual drift from format/anchor gaps; `file:line` + verbatim quote for every verdict; **exact counts (never "≈20")**; propose concrete `old → new`; **trace the full call-graph (every set-site / decorator / override / fallback), never the first grep hit**; **redact secrets** if quoting config (connection strings / tokens / keys); do NOT reorder sections, add `<!-- anchors -->` blocks, or edit owner lines.
8. **Label every fact YOU hand down by how you know it** — a fan-out agent has no way to tell your verified findings from your inferences, so an orchestrator guess arrives wearing the authority of a checked fact and propagates into whatever the agent writes. One real sweep passed down **three** wrong claims this way (a package version attributed to the wrong release, a doc said to carry markers it does not have, and three ordinary `present=ok` anchors briefed as inverted because Gate 0's own classifier had mis-bucketed them) — all three were caught only because the agents pushed back. So split the brief: facts you verified this pass, with their `file:line`, versus what you inferred or carried from a classifier, marked as such and explicitly open to being refuted. And say in the prompt that refuting you is expected: a careful agent otherwise defers to the orchestrator and writes your error into the doc. **A refutation does not make the agent right** — §10's "agent output is a lead, not a verdict" still holds both ways — it makes **your** claim the one now owing a re-verify, and until you have done that neither version goes into a doc.
9. **Project-doc agents also emit a `downstreamChanges` digest** (consumed by the domain phase and by the Phase-3 reflow, below — a section of the finder's report file, which the orchestrator reads from there since only a pointer comes back) — a short list of **flow-bearing** changes seen in the range, regardless of whether they drifted *this* doc: message topic/schema, RPC definition, shared-library version, setting trigger, routing rule, and **new/removed processing paths** (e.g. "added a batch-compliance path through a new client"). Empty is a valid answer. This is the cross-repo signal a domain flow needs but its own anchors miss. **Domain-doc agents emit the same digest for what they changed in the flow itself** (a step, participant or branch added or removed): Phase 3's `domain→project` and `domain→domain` lanes read it, and nothing else produces it.
10. **Project-doc agents also emit an `additiveCandidates` digest** (step 5 additive-candidate scan; project-doc procedure — a domain agent has no self-diff, it gets additions via the `downstreamChanges` fed to it in Phase 2) — doc-worthy facts the range INTRODUCED that this doc doesn't yet cover (not drift of existing claims): a new upstream dep, message topic / RPC definition, endpoint group / capability, background job, env / deployment / platform, cross-project seam, or a reference to a **project with no doc yet** (flag as ADD-mode candidate). Filter to the **authoring-core §0** bar ("only what code can't tell you in 10 min") — NOT every new file. Each candidate = `file:line` + one-line what-it-is + suggested section; these are **leads for the orchestrator/user to write**, the agent does NOT edit them in, and it appends them, one line each prefixed with the doc, to its own `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<date>/additive-<group>.md` rather than leaving them inside its report — scattered across reports and ledger, they once never reached the user. The orchestrator merges those files into the run's single `{{ derived.ledger_dir }}/<date>/additive.md` as each wave closes (parallel agents appending to one shared file lose lines). Empty is a valid answer. (`downstreamChanges` = flow-bearing signal for *other* docs; `additiveCandidates` = additions for *this* doc — same diff, two lenses.)

Same contract applies when `{{ names.verify }}` fans out per-doc agents over a changeset.

### Fine-scoping the deep-verify (relevance filter)

The coarse gate (step 3: 0 commits in range → skip) already drops most docs. To cheapen the rest **without false-skips**, scope WHICH claims to re-verify — but mind the failure modes (a wrong skip + the forward-moved anchor permanently buries the drift):

- **Do NOT filter on a generic "doc-relevant" include-glob.** Most behavioral facts live in ordinary source files (business logic, enums) outside config and deploy manifests — a sweep found about half its drift there. An include-glob would skip exactly those. A safe coarse denylist (range touches ONLY `*.md` / lockfiles / assets) is the only file-pattern skip that's safe; even schema files are unsafe for docs that track schema-project folders (a new project file is doc-relevant).
- **Commit messages → agent targeting hint, never a skip gate.** Feed `git log --oneline <anchor>..origin/<DEF>` into the deep-verify prompt ("these areas reportedly changed, look there"); messages lie/are vague, so they sharpen the agent, they don't decide coverage.
- **Anchor-pickaxe is the precise relevance probe (project docs).** The doc's `<!-- anchors -->` encode the exact symbols its claims depend on. For each anchor pattern, `git log -G'<pattern>' <anchor>..origin/<DEF>` tells you whether the range touched lines mentioning it. Anchor hit → re-verify that claim's area (targeted, cheap). No anchor hit anywhere in the range **and the doc's anchor coverage is good** → safe to fast-path (refresh anchor only) — record the skip on the ledger's not-touched line. This is a different axis from step 3b's existence-grep (which catches renames/removals); pickaxe catches *was-it-touched*.
- **Domain docs get a *guarded* by-age fast-path** (via the `Swept at` cursor) — no longer the blanket "always re-read". The old worry was that a cross-project flow's truth lives in the **seams between repos** that no anchor captures; that premise is now closed — §9 *mandates* seam anchors and Phase-1 emits `downstreamChanges`, so quiet seams are detectable without a full read. Fast-path a domain doc (bump `Swept at`, skip the full re-read) ONLY when **all** hold: (a) `Swept at` within **{{ audit.domain_swept_max_age_days }}d** (authoring-core §9); (b) every involved project was audited this pass (or skipped on a proven empty range) **and** none emitted `downstreamChanges` — a project that was deferred, failed or not yet reached has emitted nothing, which is not the same as nothing to emit; (c) **no** seam-anchor pickaxe hit; (d) its seam-anchor coverage is **complete** (every `Projects involved` repo + every message topic / RPC definition / shared-library / setting / routing contract is anchored). Incomplete seam coverage → does NOT qualify, full re-read. Anchor coverage is the safety lever: better seam anchors → cheaper future domain audits. This is *guarded* precisely because a domain's drift hides in seams — the guards are what make skipping safe, not an assumption that nothing changed.
- **Phased pipeline: project docs, then domain docs, then reflow.** This is how drift propagates along the cross-link graph (the bidirectional links in `references/` ARE the dependency graph), and it both saves tokens and sharpens precision:
  - **Phase 1 — project docs** (numerous, single-repo, cheap). Each agent audits its doc AND emits the `downstreamChanges` digest (contract item 9). The repo diff is read **once**, here.
  - **Phase 2 — domain docs** (few, cross-repo, otherwise the most expensive). Into each domain agent's prompt, paste the `downstreamChanges` digests of **every project under its `Projects involved`** (plus its own commit-message hints). The agent reconciles the flow narrative against those upstream changes **and** checks its own anchors — it does NOT re-diff those repos from scratch (Phase 1 already did). So a domain catches an upstream shift even when its own anchors are quiet, without paying the repo-read twice. (A real case this catches automatically: a service gained a batch-compliance path; two domain docs read "current" by their own anchors but their flow narrative had gone stale — that service's Phase-1 `downstreamChanges` flags both.)
  - **Barrier**: a domain doc runs only after its involved projects' Phase-1 results are in. A project whose result will not arrive (deferred, failed, wave reverted) does not block the domain doc: it runs without that digest, says so in its prompt, and takes a full re-read instead of the fast-path. Residual: an upstream with **no project doc** or **not listed under `Projects involved`** sends no digest → still needs the full-reread backstop, and is why `Projects involved` completeness is a quality gate.
  - **Phase 3 — reflow what Phases 1–2 disturbed: sweep the contracts, walk the links.** `downstreamChanges` is a **semantic** signal — "a service gained a batch-compliance path" — which is exactly the class of change a symbol pickaxe cannot see, so it is the cure for the backstop's residual. Phase 2 spends it in one direction only and throws the rest away. So after Phase 2: **any doc that cross-links a doc which emitted `downstreamChanges` this pass loses its fast-path and is verified**, whichever kinds the two docs are — `project→domain` (Phase 2, already done), `domain→project` (a flow shift re-opens the *other* projects under its `Projects involved`), `domain→domain` (a flow links a neighbouring flow), and `project→project` (a doc naming another project doc in Dependencies / Consumed by / Flows). The link graph is the whole of `references/`, not a bipartite one, and §6 makes it bidirectional, so it is already written down — walk it, do not re-derive it.
    **Route each change by its KIND, not by graph distance** — the digest already labels every item, and distance measures the wrong thing: a renamed message topic reaches every producer and consumer of it however many links away, while a routing tweak inside one frontend barely leaves its own doc. One hop applied to both over-reaches on the second and under-reaches on the first.
      - **Contract-shaped items** (message topic / schema, RPC definition, a shared library, a setting trigger) — **do not walk the graph. Sweep the identifier**: grep `references/` for that exact topic / service / library name, and every doc naming it is verified this pass, at any distance. One grep, exact, and it does not drag in a link-neighbour that never mentioned the thing. §9 already mandates seam anchors for precisely these contracts, so the identifier to grep is written down rather than guessed.
        **A shared library fires on a contract change, never on a version number.** A widely named library is bumped routinely; sweeping on the bump alone would re-open most docs on most runs, which is the blow-up failure arriving through the lane that was supposed to be the cheap one. So: the exported surface changed (a renamed or removed component / type, a breaking change) → sweep; a version bump with no surface change → record it on the not-touched line and stop. This also closes the residual above: an upstream with no project doc, or missing from a `Projects involved` list, sends no digest — but a doc that *names* the topic is still caught, because the sweep does not depend on the link being recorded.
      - **Local-shaped items** (a routing rule, a processing path added or removed inside one service) — **one hop along the cross-link graph**, which is the whole of `references/` and bidirectional per §6: `domain→project`, `domain→domain` and `project→project` as much as the `project→domain` Phase 2 already did. These only bear on the docs that describe that service's behaviour, and a fixpoint expansion from one busy repo would re-open most of the kb — the blow-up failure wearing a graph. The second hop lands on the next pass, bounded by the {{ audit.full_sweep_max_age_days }}d backstop.
      Record on the ledger which docs each lane re-opened and which emitter pulled each in — a doc dragged in this way looks identical to one that qualified on its own, and you want to be able to tell whether the reflow is earning its cost.
- **Anchor the seams.** Cross-project contracts (message topics, RPC definitions, shared libraries, setting triggers, routing) MUST be anchored; a change in a shared/contract repo triggers re-verify of every flow riding it. Anchor coverage is the lever — the more complete, the cheaper+safer every future audit.
- **Backstop (the residual) — {{ audit.full_sweep_max_age_days }} days, at two levels.** Pickaxe is symbol-level; transitive/upstream behavior drift escapes it, and so does a change that rewrites a flow without touching any anchored symbol. Time is the only thing that catches those, so (authoring-core §9): a **domain doc** whose `Swept at` is older than **{{ audit.domain_swept_max_age_days }}d** is force-read in full, whatever its anchors say; and a **run** starting more than **{{ audit.full_sweep_max_age_days }}d** after the last full sweep runs in **full mode** outright. The per-run level exists because a project doc has no field recording when it was last read whole — `Verified at` advances on every pass, fast-pathed ones included — so a per-doc backstop cannot be evaluated for one without inventing a field. The project-doc **empty-commit-range** skip survives full mode either way: git proving 0 commits is a fact, not a heuristic. Any doc whose anchors don't cover its claims has no backstop at all — re-read it every pass until coverage is complete.

## Hard rules (these caused real corrections — keep them)

- **Ownership only from the user** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §6`). A code/deploy/catalog value that disagrees with the doc's `- Owner:` → surface it for the user, leave the line as-is.
- **Redact all secrets** — never copy passwords / tokens / keys / webhooks / connection strings, even if pasted.
- **Environment tables list only {{ lists.env_names }}**{{#if has.excluded_envs}} — never {{ lists.env_excluded }}; filter it out of any manifest diff{{/if}}.
- **`- Audience:` header carries the exposure/internal classification** ({{ lists.audiences_deployed }}; {{ lists.audiences_artifact }}; ` + `-joined for a split repo; authoring-core §3/§6). It is **derived** from the doc's own `Domains/ingress` + purpose (public ingress → the public slug; internal ingress or a back-office/ops purpose → back-office/internal-service) — not code-diffable on its own, so only flag it when the ingress it derives from actually changed. Don't re-annotate "internal-only" in prose; the header field is the single home.
- **A doc having grown is NOT a finding.** Never propose splitting a doc on length alone; the three-condition gate and the decision-fork-vs-enumeration-fork split test live in `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §2` — read it there, don't re-derive a threshold here.
- State facts from code; do **not** assert speculative bugs (a DI/config branch is a fact; "this is a prod bug" is a guess — omit it).
{{#if has.logs}}
- **Verify log values with a live query**, don't trust config (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §5`). Declarative no-person prose; markers per `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §0` (unmarked = verified, **no bare `(verified)`, no emoji**; `(unverified)`; `(human:)`; `(external: <team>)`; counter-intuitive facts stated plainly); large detail points to source.
{{else}}
- Declarative no-person prose; markers per `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §0` (unmarked = verified, **no bare `(verified)`, no emoji**; `(unverified)`; `(human:)`; `(external: <team>)`; counter-intuitive facts stated plainly); large detail points to source.
{{/if}}
- **`(human: who, date)` facts are code-invisible — never code-diff them.** They can't be confirmed against a repo (that's the point). Surface the oldest by date for the orchestrator to re-confirm with the named contributor (same treatment as `noncode refs`). A code diff that **contradicts** a `(human:)` fact → flag for human review, do not auto-edit.
- **`(external: <team>)` facts are cross-team-owned — never code-diff them either.** Authority is another team's code/settings we don't track or reach; a full clone still can't resolve them, and the live tools usually can't reach their settings. Treat like `noncode refs`: surface the oldest by `verified:` date for manual cross-team re-check. Unlike `(unverified)`, this never becomes resolvable here — don't downgrade it to `(unverified)`.
- **Retired entries** (a row in the `## Retired` section of `project/index.md` + residue maps): **don't flag the repo state as drift** — whether it is archived, gone entirely, or still carrying live-looking CI deploy jobs, none of that is a finding for something nobody runs. Re-verify only that each **KEEP** residue still lives where the map says (a live redirect that got removed IS drift); **REMOVE** items disappearing is the goal — drop those rows / the whole block once all REMOVE items are gone.

## After writing

**Run the mechanical self-verify before reporting** — the procedure is `../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10` ("a correction you just made is itself a new claim" + the proxy probe), and the anchor half is §9 pilot mode. Sync-local's own part is **where it sits and what it gates**: per-claim verification already happened inside each wave (the wave gate), so this closing pass is the **cross-doc** half only — the ledger reconciles against the run's full diff, and the within-doc contradictions a per-wave view structurally cannot see. It is a stage of the sweep, not a favour, and the closing report's `done` bucket may not be written until it has. That placement is what makes "is there a bug?" answerable with "verified, none" rather than surfacing new ones each time the user asks — per §10, the question arising at all means the contract was not kept.

The review of the touched `references/` files is this subset, not a prompt audit (the skill-file criteria of a prompt review are mostly N/A on fact docs): every relative link resolves; no net bullet loss per section against the run's base; the banned-wording grep (§0 markers — no bare `(verified)`, no emoji); and a read for wording the edit left dangling (an orphaned "also" / "now", a stale provenance date).

Then summarize per doc: **unchanged / updated (what) / flagged (why) / additive candidates (what the range introduced to consider adding)** + coverage gaps. The additive candidates come from `additive.md` (deduped by doc + candidate, since a same-date re-run appends to the same file) — list them in the report, or link the file when the list runs long; a run whose report omits them has dropped them. Show the diff; **do not commit unless the user asks** (`knowledge: sync <scope> — <summary>`, technical-only).

**Terminate, don't trickle** (`../{{ names.authoring_core }}.md §10`): the closing report is exactly three parts — ① **done** / ② **irreducible-open** (the manifest's `(external:)` / `(human:)` / preference rows, no user action unless they override) / ③ **one next action** (usually push) — with **no "anything else to verify?" menu**. The per-doc summary above (unchanged / updated / flagged + coverage gaps) is this sweep's `done` bucket, and **the ledger is what makes it checkable** — name its path in the report, and say how many rows it holds against how many changed claims the diff carries (claims, not lines — the next-session check above says why). Those two numbers are the claim that `done` means done; a reader who has them does not need to ask for the sweep to be re-verified.
